**Gate results (batch us046):**
- Windows: `ValidateVendor`, `win-dev`, `win-debug`, and `win-unit` (21/21) pass. `gpu dx12` passes 44 of 44 cases (17,832 assertions). `gpu vulkan` passes 45 of 45 cases (17,844 assertions).
- Linux: `ValidateVendor`, `linux-dev`, `linux-debug` (0 warnings), `linux-unit` (22/22), `linux-gcc-debug`, `linux-gcc-unit` (22/22), `linux-asan`, and `linux-sanitize` (22/22) pass. The `engine_tests --suite unit` run has 497 cases, all passing.
- Not run: the native Linux Vulkan GPU suite (no usable device on this WSL host), `engine_bench --suite acceptance` (no bench target until US-074), a GUI check, and GitHub CI. I did not push or commit.

**What was built:**
- The scene pass writes linear HDR when `SceneRendererDesc::post` is on. Jitter is applied to the rasterized position only, so motion vectors stay unjittered.
- `PostProcessor` runs TAA with ping-ponged history, a 9-tap separable bloom, an ACES tone map with manual EV100 exposure, the sRGB encode, and the overlay composited last. It runs as compute passes in one render graph.
- History resets on first frame, resize, camera cut, and teleport. The GPU uses the same reset rule as the CPU reference.
- The GPU suite compares output against the CPU reference pixel by pixel. It covers NaN/Inf/negative inputs, overlay independence at two exposures, a moving block's history, and the scene path with post on.

**Decisions you should know about:**
- Post is opt-in (`post.enabled`, default off). With it off, the 8-bit path is unchanged, so the existing golden and material fixtures stay as they were.
- Exposure is manual EV100 on the GPU. The automatic luminance-based exposure exists in the CPU reference only.
- The post pass runs at full resolution, with no upscale. That is US-046.
- Alpha-blended items write no motion vector, so moving transparent objects can ghost.
- The scene-level far-camera checks verify that the scene draws real entities, not a center pixel. At 110 units the near box is under one pixel, so a center-pixel check there was testing sampling luck. I confirmed the plain renderer draws the same frame, so this is a test change, not a renderer fix.

**Notes:**
- The first sanitizer run had one failed integration case. The isolated rerun and the full second run both passed with zero sanitizer reports, so I'm treating it as the known X11 focus flake.
- The progress log has two US-039 entries. The earlier partial one is still there as history, and the new one at the end supersedes it.
